WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.938
An Ollama installation should be treated as a managed service, not just a one-time tool download.

2
00:00:05.938 --> 00:00:14.701
Teams should know how it was installed, how the service starts, how it stops, which user account it runs under, and where its runtime state lives.

3
00:00:14.701 --> 00:00:23.753
Those details matter when troubleshooting a failed startup, a missing model, a permission problem, or a configuration mismatch between machines.

4
00:00:23.753 --> 00:00:29.909
Service lifecycle practices are especially important when a local tool becomes part of shared work.

5
00:00:29.909 --> 00:00:35.992
A developer workstation, a lab server, and a production-like host may all need different settings.

6
00:00:35.992 --> 00:00:38.309
A workstation may favor flexibility.

7
00:00:38.309 --> 00:00:45.986
A shared server may need predictable startup, controlled network exposure, documented storage, and support expectations.

8
00:00:45.986 --> 00:00:50.620
A production-like host may require tighter change control and monitoring.

9
00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:58.007
Environment variables and service configuration should be documented because small differences can change behavior.

10
00:00:58.007 --> 00:01:05.756
Storage locations, bind addresses, model cache behavior, runtime directories, and service users can all affect operations.

11
00:01:05.756 --> 00:01:12.998
If one machine behaves differently from another, the answer is often in configuration rather than in the model itself.

12
00:01:12.998 --> 00:01:19.226
Logs and service status checks are the first place to look when the runtime does not behave as expected.

13
00:01:19.226 --> 00:01:24.874
Clear lifecycle practices make upgrades, restarts, troubleshooting, and support easier.

14
00:01:24.874 --> 00:01:30.957
They also help new team members understand the environment without depending on tribal knowledge.

15
00:01:30.957 --> 00:01:36.534
It is also helpful to separate development assumptions from shared-service assumptions.

16
00:01:36.534 --> 00:01:42.979
A developer may restart a local service freely, change settings for a test, or remove models to save space.

17
00:01:42.979 --> 00:01:51.814
A shared host needs a more predictable process so active users, dependent applications, and support staff understand what is changing and when.
